  "title": "A regular zero-area singularity with a nonzero weak Bianchi defect",
  "description": "<p>We give a counterexample to the geometric regular-zero-area-singularity implication in the Oberwolfach report conjecture (problem 30004807 / OWR-8415343-010), under the cited Burtscher-Kiessling-Tahvildar-Zadeh convention of smooth compactly supported vector fields reaching the retained inner boundary and regularity of the static spacelike slices. The spacetime is a negative-mass specialization of the known Dadhich-Kar-Mukherjee-Visser metric, whose tensor formulas are credited. Its static slices have harmonically regular zero-area singularities of mass -2a. We prove absolute convergence of the complete weak Bianchi scalar for every permitted test and compute an explicit nonzero boundary functional, with value -8 pi for a normalized radial test. The boundary-flux method is credited to the existing literature; convergence is established directly, without invoking a sufficient theorem outside its hypotheses.</p><p>The example violates the null energy condition. Added energy conditions, prescribed matter models, arbitrary tilted-slice regularity, collapsed-point test fields and the wider matter/evolution program are outside the claim. No new metric, new tensor formula, new general boundary theorem, historical first priority or continued-openness certificate is claimed. The priority search is extensive but bounded.</p><p>Unrefereed research note, with no independent human peer review. AI tools were used extensively in solving, drafting, independently auditing, checking computations and preparing the materials. AI-assisted reviews are not formal verification or human peer review. The deposit includes a self-contained analytic proof and a portable verification archive with preserved historical inputs and independent reproducible consistency checks; finite checks do not prove quantified conclusions.</p>",
